# Scheduling for better data locality and efficiency.
#
# The main issue in the baseline kernel is poor spatial locality: for a fixed
# column k and j>k, the inner loops iterate over i while accessing A[i, j]
# and Q[i, k]. Since A and Q are laid out row-major as A[m][n], varying i
# with fixed j (or k) steps by a stride of n in memory.
#
# We transform the loops so that, for the expensive update phase (j>k),
# iteration is over i outside and j inside:
#
#   for j in ...:
#       R[k,j] = 0
#   for i in ...:
#       for j in ...:
#           R[k,j] += Q[i,k] * A[i,j]
#   for i in ...:
#       for j in ...:
#           A[i,j] -= Q[i,k] * R[k,j]
#
# This preserves the mathematical computation while:
#   - Making the innermost loop iterate over j, which is the contiguous
#     dimension (improves locality for A[i, j]).
#   - Reusing Q[i, k] across many inner-loop iterations so the C compiler
#     can keep it in a register.
